# Readio SSMD Prosody Control Showcase

:::{voice="narrator"}
This example demonstrates volume, rate, and pitch controls supported by SSMD and rendered by Readio through PyKokoro.

First, here is the neutral reference phrase.
The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.
:::

## Volume: named levels

:::{voice="narrator"}
Each changed phrase uses the same sentence so the level is easier to compare.

Extra soft.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{volume="x-soft"}

Soft.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{volume="soft"}

Medium.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{volume="medium"}

Loud.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{volume="loud"}

Extra loud.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{volume="x-loud"}

Silent.
[You should not hear this sentence.]{volume="silent"}
:::

## Rate: named levels

:::{voice="narrator"}
Extra slow.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{rate="x-slow"}

Slow.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{rate="slow"}

Medium.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{rate="medium"}

Fast.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{rate="fast"}

Extra fast.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{rate="x-fast"}
:::

## Pitch: named levels

:::{voice="narrator"}
Extra low.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{pitch="x-low"}

Low.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{pitch="low"}

Medium.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{pitch="medium"}

High.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{pitch="high"}

Extra high.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{pitch="x-high"}
:::

## Numeric and named values

:::{voice="narrator"}
Volume accepts numeric levels. Rate and pitch use named values or percentages.

Volume level two.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{volume="2"}

Volume level four.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{volume="4"}

Slower rate.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{rate="slow"}

Faster rate.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{rate="fast"}

Lower pitch.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{pitch="low"}

Higher pitch.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{pitch="high"}
:::

## Relative values

:::{voice="narrator"}
Volume can be changed with decibels.

Six decibels louder.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{volume="+6dB"}

Three decibels quieter.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{volume="-3dB"}

Rate can use relative percentages.

Twenty percent faster.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{rate="+20%"}

Ten percent slower.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{rate="-10%"}

Pitch can also use relative percentages.

Ten percent higher.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{pitch="+10%"}

Ten percent lower.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{pitch="-10%"}
:::

## Combined inline prosody

:::{voice="narrator"}
Now all three controls are combined.

Loud, fast, and high.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{volume="loud" rate="fast" pitch="high"}

Soft, slow, and low.
[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]{volume="soft" rate="slow" pitch="low"}
:::

## Block-level prosody

:::{voice="narrator"}
The next paragraph applies prosody to a complete directive block.
:::

:::{voice="narrator" volume="loud" rate="slow" pitch="low"}
This whole block is loud, slow, and low pitched.
A second sentence inherits the same prosody settings.
:::

## Inline override inside a block

:::{voice="narrator" volume="soft" rate="slow" pitch="low"}
This sentence inherits soft, slow, and low settings.

[This phrase overrides all three settings and becomes loud, fast, and high.]{volume="loud" rate="fast" pitch="high"}

This sentence returns to the surrounding block settings.
:::

:::{voice="narrator"}
That concludes the Readio SSMD prosody showcase.
:::
